docs/book/src/concepts/agent-loop.md:45:- `ExecuteTools { calls }` — fan out the tool calls, feed outcomes back as `TransitionInput::ToolResults`.
docs/book/src/concepts/agent-loop.md:49:A typical run walks `CallingModel → ExecutingTools → CallingModel → … → Done`. Each tool round bumps `turn`; reaching `RunConfig::max_turns` fails the run with `AgentError::MaxTurnsExceeded`. With an `output_type` configured, the loop adds a `Finalizing` (and, on a validation miss, one `RepairingOutput`) turn before `Done`. See [Structured Output](./structured-output-builder.md) for that path.
docs/book/src/concepts/agent-loop.md:51:A model turn can end with `FinishReason::Length` or `FinishReason::ContentFilter` and still hold tool calls. The provider cut that turn, so the tool call arguments can be incomplete. The loop fails the run with `AgentError::ToolCallsNotRun`. No tool call and no handoff from that turn runs. A stream that ended early with tool calls and no `Finish` event fails in the same way, with `finish_reason: None`. A custom `Model` must emit `Finish`.
docs/book/src/concepts/agent-loop.md:69:- Terminal: `RunCompleted { usage }`, `RunFailed { error }`, `RunDeferred { calls, usage }`, `RunStopped { reason, usage }`. A run ends with exactly one of these. `RunDeferred` means the run stopped on tool calls that wait for a host decision. `RunStopped` means the host stopped the run at a turn boundary through the turn checkpoint. A cancelled run can end its stream with no terminal event. A runner such as `TokioRunner` reports this as `RunError::Cancelled`. If you wrap the `Agent::run` stream in `RunResultStreaming` with no runner, `RunResultStreaming::collect` returns `Ok`.
docs/book/src/concepts/agent-loop.md:132:- `parallel_tool_call_limit: Option<NonZeroUsize>` — cap on concurrently-executing tool calls. `None` = unbounded. Honored by the core loop driver.
docs/book/src/concepts/agent-loop.md:149:Cancellation is *not* a `RunConfig` field — the canonical `CancellationToken` lives on `RunContext`. Cancellation and timeout are best-effort: a genuine terminal event that already occurred wins over a late cancel or timeout, so a caller cannot assume that calling `cancel()` always yields `RunError::Cancelled`.
docs/book/src/concepts/agent-loop.md:195:The checkpoint does not remove the item from the inbox. The host removes it when it sees `UserMessageInjected` in the event stream (see "Commit rule" below). If a guardrail rejects the item, or a cancel ends the run first, the item stays in the inbox. This example needs `Runner::run_streamed`. The stream gives `UserMessageInjected` to the host before the loop reaches the next boundary. `Runner::run` returns the events only when the run ends, so with `run` this checkpoint injects the same item again at each boundary.
